@page "/components/file-upload"

<PageTitle>File Upload — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">File Upload</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    A drop zone that posts the file on its own and keeps the URL. shadcn has no file field, and a bare <code>&lt;input type="file"&gt;</code> is not one: it renders as a button whose label the page cannot write, it shows a filename and never a picture, and it posts the bytes — which forces the whole surrounding form to be multipart and to handle the upload itself.
</p>

<p class="doc-note">
    What the form carries is a hidden input holding the URL that came back. That buys three things:
    the surrounding form stays an ordinary form, a chosen image survives a validation failure that
    re-renders the page, and the field can sit inside a form that is already doing something else.
</p>
<Example Source="FileUploadExample.razor"><FileUploadExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">With a value</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The picture is the control. The only thing drawn on top of it is the way to remove it, and it
    appears with the pointer — as a word underneath, that button is a permanent offer to delete
    something that is usually not there.
</p>
<Example Source="FileUploadFilled.razor"><FileUploadFilled /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Not a picture</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>ChildContent</code> replaces the preview and the prompt. Everything underneath is
    unchanged — drop, choose, post, keep the URL — so a document, an avatar or a list of names
    are all the same component wearing different clothes.
</p>
<Example Source="FileUploadDocument.razor"><FileUploadDocument /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">The endpoint</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Anything that accepts a multipart <code>file</code> and answers with a JSON object carrying a
    <code>url</code>. Everything else in the answer is ignored, so an endpoint may say more. The
    antiforgery token is read from the surrounding form and sent with the file, because ASP.NET
    refuses the POST otherwise — and it refuses with a bodyless 400, which reads like a broken
    endpoint rather than a missing token.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs" Class="mt-4 max-w-2xl" Code="@Endpoint" />

<h2 class="doc-h2">Refusing a file</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>Accept</code> is given to the picker AND checked before the upload, because a picker's
    accept is advice: a file dragged onto the zone never went through it. <code>MaxBytes</code> is
    checked in the same place, so a file that is too large is refused without being sent.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">RTL</h2>
<Example Source="FileUploadRtl.razor"><FileUploadRtl /></Example>

@code {
    const string Endpoint = """
        app.MapPost("/uploads", async (IFormFile file, IStorage storage) =>
        {
            var url = await storage.SaveAsync(file);
            return Results.Ok(new { url });
        }).DisableAntiforgery();   // or keep it, and the field sends the token
        """;
}
